    MetaInformation -> <|"XMP" -> <||>|>],
   Selectable->False],
  DefaultBaseStyle->"ImageGraphics",
  ImageSizeRaw->{921., 314.},
  PlotRange->{{0, 921.}, {0, 314.}}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9628621656601677`*^9, 
  3.9628621695612173`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"52699343-b7c0-461c-be5b-\
a612dc75af17"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So this can be thought of as a very engineering and creative process, that \
might heavily depend on the problem that needs to be solved. Here, we have \
some mathematical object ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["z", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "be8fa708-92a3-4fac-8246-a43e6ffee4dd"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"X", "(", "z", ")"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Y", "(", "z", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "54393204-c69a-4e15-ad8c-596f909c2a58"],
 " might be 2 correlated features. Then, we can finda  regression function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     OverscriptBox["f", "^"], "(", 
     RowBox[{"X", "(", "z", ")"}], ")"}], " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Y", "(", "z", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "ac742f47-9f17-485d-8ee3-c402c22ea8b8"],
 ". Now, if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   OverscriptBox["f", "^"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "9387c07f-293a-43c1-9ea1-e95d689aaa72"],
 "m turns out to be more accurate than we would expect, that means that there \
may be an actual relationship, which is often exact in mathematics. Then \
attribution techniques can be used to understand which components of the \
input ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["X", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "0c485da5-16d6-432b-a02d-ace516d8f804"],
 " might actually be relevant. These will involve classic statistical \
techniques. The technique paper used is called ",
 StyleBox["gradient saliency",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", where we check the gradient of output with respect to the input, and if \
it is high, we keep that input and if not, we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t. This \
helps the mathematician."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.962861653012293*^9, {3.962862498106769*^9, 3.962862498812313*^9}, {
   3.962862549301248*^9, 3.962862566729041*^9}, {3.962864289867592*^9, 
   3.962864302730147*^9}, {3.962864377845606*^9, 3.962864419288403*^9}, {
   3.962864469921453*^9, 3.9628646597985497`*^9}, {3.962868427615231*^9, 
   3.96286845314607*^9}, {3.962868485116302*^9, 
   3.9628685400511703`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fd9917b2-7baf-4822-955a-\
43aeac95fd2c"],

Cell["\<\
So here, the first problem tackled is a knot theory problem. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96286857476258*^9, 3.9628685864618673`*^9}, {
  3.9628687404480057`*^9, 3.9628687405526867`*^9}, {3.962868797688711*^9, 
  3.962868809965859*^9}, {3.962870146341771*^9, 
  3.962870150494011*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c947d64a-eb86-4e51-9ac3-\
9f478e0dd0b3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Note. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Volume conjecture in knot theory: hyperbolic volume of a knot (geometric \
invariant) should be encoded within the asymptotic behaviour of its coloured \
Jones polynomials (algebraic invariant). "
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962870154763344*^9, 3.9628701957639437`*^9}, {
  3.962870293666235*^9, 3.9628703109230633`*^9}, {3.962872641632777*^9, 
  3.962872643297827*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"316bf6b1-411b-4bb9-8a7c-\
f84879b785cc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "These guys studied how geometric invariants of knots influence ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "K", ")"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "6adcd6fd-983f-4668-88b6-f52478b2cf09"],
 ", called the signature of the knot, which is thought to not be related to \
the hyperbolic geometry. But there turned out to be some correlation. Using \
the attribution technique, three invariants of the cusp geometry turned out \
to be most useful. These are the meridional translation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[Mu]", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "54423de0-325e-4ca4-aef8-176a2c560c3f"],
 " and the longitudinal translation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[Lambda]", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "673cc8ee-c28d-41ee-8526-c0b46a29055f"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962870416589098*^9, 3.962870502049417*^9}, {
  3.962870612523329*^9, 3.962870634736085*^9}, {3.962870789620247*^9, 
  3.9628707913191633`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"38f0baa7-b2e0-478a-acb4-\
c7dfc6e144e6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Note. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Their initial conjecture: there exists constants ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["c", "1"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "3a90fa29-a2ed-4372-a86f-d056c563c492"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["c", "2"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "5541157b-720f-4bde-82f3-04b5009370e3"],
 " such that for hyperbolic knot ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["K", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "cd2e6813-edae-472e-8e2a-d6b666a94702"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"|", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", " ", 
      RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "K", ")"}]}], " ", "-", " ", 
     RowBox[{"slope", "(", "K", ")"}]}], "|", " ", 
    RowBox[{"<", " ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["c", "1"], 
       RowBox[{"vol", "(", "K", ")"}]}], " ", "+", " ", 
      SubscriptBox["c", "2"]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "b76a5dfe-2305-45ec-b208-f6257c0db8d5"],
 ". "
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962870154763344*^9, 3.9628701917152042`*^9}, {
  3.9628704134019203`*^9, 3.962870414584114*^9}, {3.9628705525746527`*^9, 
  3.96287059646218*^9}, {3.9628707980468807`*^9, 3.962870830676113*^9}, {
  3.962870980231962*^9, 
  3.96287098023387*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"79957ae6-28e2-4c54-a397-\
aa7ced443542"],

Cell["\<\
This conjecture is not true and they found counterexample. But this motivated \
the following theorem:\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962870154763344*^9, 3.9628701917152042`*^9}, {
   3.9628704134019203`*^9, 3.962870414584114*^9}, {3.9628705525746527`*^9, 
   3.96287059646218*^9}, {3.9628707980468807`*^9, 3.962870830676113*^9}, {
   3.962870980731216*^9, 3.962871008542096*^9}, {3.962871114037784*^9, 
   3.962871115187252*^9}, 3.962871204204597*^9, {3.962871302095503*^9, 
   3.962871320406905*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"38a3d7af-5e82-499b-bd9f-\
8572a78349ce"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " There exists constants ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["c", "1"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "5a9ee638-4fa9-4146-b297-512a7d63fcf1"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["c", "2"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "d8e015fd-20ed-4b43-acb5-a9e6f413dbfe"],
 " such that, for every hyperbolic knot ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["K", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "4162442f-6cf1-497c-acaa-5326fdc04aac"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"|", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", " ", 
      RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "K", ")"}]}], " ", "-", " ", 
     RowBox[{"slope", "(", "K", ")"}]}], "|", " ", 
    RowBox[{"<=", " ", 
     RowBox[{"c", " ", 
      RowBox[{"vol", "(", "K", ")"}], " ", 
      SuperscriptBox[
       RowBox[{"inj", "(", "K", ")"}], 
       RowBox[{"-", "3"}]]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "cf292cd5-9eb3-4288-9cfb-10ea80f21943"]
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962870154763344*^9, 3.9628701917152042`*^9}, {
   3.9628704134019203`*^9, 3.962870414584114*^9}, {3.9628705525746527`*^9, 
   3.96287059646218*^9}, {3.9628707980468807`*^9, 3.962870830676113*^9}, {
   3.962870980731216*^9, 3.962871008542096*^9}, {3.962871114037784*^9, 
   3.962871115187252*^9}, 3.962871204204597*^9, {3.962871302095503*^9, 
   3.962871322805438*^9}, {3.962871571160303*^9, 3.962871612962493*^9}, {
   3.962871830398553*^9, 
   3.962871830400134*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3688f5e3-7c5e-44bf-9490-\
995efc4d3f87"],

Cell["\<\
Can be used to do some E8 stuff over here! So yeah. That is a good open \
project because it is related to so many other things. But let\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] keep things easy for now.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962871693562914*^9, 3.9628717081562243`*^9}, {
  3.962871814133689*^9, 
  3.962871828573532*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e08fba85-f22b-4f3d-9fab-\
2e1f3d24db0b"],

Cell["\<\
Then these guys tackled a representation theory problem. They studied these \
so called KL polynomials. And these turned out to be related to bruhat \
graphs. So it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s very nice applciation of ML where \
attribution techniques were used to determine important features of the \
graph. Then they did some stuff over here.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9628719287389183`*^9, 3.962871963711047*^9}, {
  3.962872459913843*^9, 3.962872472407721*^9}, {3.962872503545607*^9, 
  3.962872546498094*^9}, {3.962872594078964*^9, 
  3.9628726027158613`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fbee2c80-b262-44f7-9850-\
8cdf9637bca5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Every bruhat interval admits a canonical hypercube decomposition along its \
extremal reflections, from which the KL polynomial is directly computable."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962872607311153*^9, 
  3.9628726390089617`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a10ffb78-b5b7-44dc-be6a-\
dde07d43441e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Conclusion", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962872931281739*^9, 
  3.962872936362249*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c4062693-c48c-4929-b72a-\
886fc80a0f88"],

Cell["\<\
Rather than using machine learning to directly generate conjectures, we focus \
on helping guide the intuition off expert mathematicians. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96287300630643*^9, 
  3.962873045260996*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"706d262e-7ae2-42b8-93f0-\
5939cd13a98c"],

Cell["\<\
My take is that LLMs are now just gonna take over the mathematical world as \
well. Because they are better at English than humans. So they can better at \
mathematics than humans as well. Because in pure mathematics, often data is \
not that available especially if we are dealing in abstract areas like rings \
and stuff where one needs to prove things very quickly (maybe we can \
algorithmically enumerate a subset of rings and test properties on them \
etc.). But often they are not easy to test, often incomputable. So maybe AI \
can develop the intuition in the first place and actually prove a lot of \
theorems. Because today pure math seems to be a slow field. I think since it \
has been formalized so much, such intuition can be achieved? \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962873110714019*^9, 3.962873250245405*^9}, {
  3.962873581097614*^9, 3.962873649274282*^9}, {3.963122881335353*^9, 
  3.963122881903109*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f6708737-8017-4b8b-aaa6-\
e53939116127"],

Cell["\<\
Formalization of a concrete problem? Right now, I don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t know \
what project to do. Let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s read and learn more. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96287373611308*^9, 3.962873745286951*^9}, {
  3.963122961129079*^9, 
  3.963122975450666*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e0cd1ec6-36dc-4c0a-923d-\
f37e4af2f559"],

Cell["\<\
Just being good at an area of mathematics? We can ask vatsal/pranjal to work \
on an NLP project. It will be fun to work on. And he works at netflix as \
well. Can get a referral over here. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962873749940301*^9, 3.962873757719224*^9}, {
  3.9639822794508*^9, 
  3.963982307671412*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"57488d3d-4d75-447a-8199-\
99e86addc960"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Methods", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9628737780562468`*^9, 
  3.962873780064227*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c260fd81-7cdc-45de-adae-\
cd213275eed8"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Constructions in combinatorics via neural networks", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.962861575511447*^9, 
  3.962861582391506*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9903eff5-ee64-401a-a749-\
5ad1189417c0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963982001944057*^9, 
  3.9639820139848948`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"aaafff1d-0322-4d8f-82f7-\
4ec87e89801f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Using a reinforcement learning algorithm, the deep cross",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "b2df43a7-13f8-4bf7-9d0e-1b4246493751"],
 "entropy method, one can find explicit constructions and counterexamples to \
several open conjectures in extremal combinatorics and graph theory. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963982026403626*^9, 
  3.96398207884021*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1e454449-4dcd-4956-838e-\
f99e0f534d86"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963982133655546*^9, 
  3.963982135519074*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9f2c2a33-0999-4616-a790-\
2679b3381c99"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We have an agent that does most of the work for us. The agent starts the \
learning process with zero prior knowledge about the problem itself. \
Generates a construction/solution and receives feedback from a scoring \
function on how well it did.  Eventually, does some gradient descent/ascent \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "6a77e6e9-331b-4fb7-919f-3c2770882ddc"],
 " this is called reinforcement learning. Repeating this process many times, \
the agent eventually learns how to get a better score in this ",
 StyleBox["\[OpenCurlyQuote]game\[CloseCurlyQuote].",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " If this score is larger than the score of the conjectured best \
construction, then we have succeeded in finding a counterexample to the \
conjecture"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9639821367385607`*^9, 3.963982174023286*^9}, {
  3.9639823338869457`*^9, 
  3.9639824284129868`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ecd419f1-d846-4ef7-8e85-\
13620805a88e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s creating a scoring function itself base don the \
parameters? We have deep ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"Q", "-"}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "1513ad97-d664-41a8-9162-e74344325ad9"],
 " networks. Also, the way we present the problem is also important as we can \
express it as a linear program etc."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96398266352101*^9, 3.9639826892243433`*^9}, {
  3.963982821320006*^9, 3.963982836461184*^9}, {3.9639835062944937`*^9, 
  3.963983512380393*^9}, {3.9639842300024652`*^9, 
  3.9639842304393797`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ceb6cd3d-e36a-4585-8dde-\
43fbab2bf783"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Given any state as an input to the neural net, the output is a probability \
distribution on all the\npossible moves in that state, with higher \
probability assigned to the moves that the agent thinks are\nbest. This is \
the general algorithm for the deep cross",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "81f5d276-0012-4e81-9e27-801d31952203"],
 "entropy method:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.963984204005748*^9, 3.963984218360593*^9}, {
  3.963984573129249*^9, 3.9639845999888906`*^9}, {3.963984633208686*^9, 
  3.963984646783038*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0a7a04b8-5ca3-44d7-a992-\
41007628cb70"],

